# TCP probe targets — <IP> <port> <interval-seconds> <label> <role>
#
# Copy to config/tcp-targets.conf. Addresses below are RFC 5737 documentation
# addresses and will not work anywhere.
#
# ==========================================================================
# THIS IS NOT A PORT SCAN
# ==========================================================================
#
# Each line opens ONE connection to ONE known port at a fixed, slow interval,
# to measure whether a TCP handshake completes and how long it takes. It never
# enumerates ports and never touches a host that is not listed here.
#
# The distinction matters operationally, not just ethically: a scan on somebody
# else's production network is an incident, and you will spend the rest of the
# investigation explaining it instead of doing it.
#
# ==========================================================================
# WHY TCP IN ADDITION TO ICMP
# ==========================================================================
#
# ICMP tells you whether packets cross. It does not tell you whether a session
# survives. Some faults drop sessions while individual packets keep flowing —
# and some drop packets without a single session noticing. Measuring both is
# how you tell those apart, and in the campaign this came from, the two
# genuinely decoupled: an outage that took the gateway away for five minutes
# left every LAN target at zero loss.
#
# ==========================================================================
# THE INTERVAL IS NOT A PERFORMANCE SETTING
# ==========================================================================
#
# Probe slowly enough that you do not change what you are measuring.
#
# The concrete case that set the 15-second interval below: every connection to
# a Windows Remote Desktop port writes an entry to that host's own connection
# log, and that log is a ring buffer. Probing it every two seconds would have
# rotated away the very evidence the investigation depended on — the tool would
# have destroyed its own case, slowly, while appearing to work.
#
# Ask, for every target: what does connecting to this cost the target?

# <IP>        <port>  <interval>  <label>        <role>
192.0.2.20    3389    15          ts01-rdp       symptom
192.0.2.20    445     2           ts01-smb       symptom-alt
192.0.2.44    1433    2           sql01-mssql    other-host
192.0.2.45    22      2           scan01-ssh     fabric-ref
192.0.2.33    22      2           probe3-ssh     fabric-ref
